{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A **process-per-turn** CLI (one prompt, exit, `--resume` for the next turn) is simpler to reason about: each invocation has a clean process boundary, predictable resource cleanup, and easy parallel runs or scripting, but you pay cold-start latency, lose in-process context unless you persist and reload state on every resume, and mid-run steering (cancel, inject, redirect) needs another mechanism because the process is already gone or not listening.\n\nA stdin-open long-lived CLI keeps the agent warm, so follow-up turns are fast, cancellation and live user input map naturally to the same session, and you can stream partial output while still accepting input; the tradeoff is a heavier, stateful process (hangs, memory growth, stuck locks), trickier lifecycle in scripts and CI, and more work to make shutdown, signal handling, and concurrent sessions safe.\n\nIn practice, process-per-turn fits automation, batch jobs, and \u201cone shot per shell command\u201d; persistent stdin fits interactive REPL-style use where the human stays in one session and may interrupt or steer without restarting."}]},"session_id":"11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":5962,"duration_api_ms":5962,"is_error":false,"result":"A **process-per-turn** CLI (one prompt, exit, `--resume` for the next turn) is simpler to reason about: each invocation has a clean process boundary, predictable resource cleanup, and easy parallel runs or scripting, but you pay cold-start latency, lose in-process context unless you persist and reload state on every resume, and mid-run steering (cancel, inject, redirect) needs another mechanism because the process is already gone or not listening.\n\nA stdin-open long-lived CLI keeps the agent warm, so follow-up turns are fast, cancellation and live user input map naturally to the same session, and you can stream partial output while still accepting input; the tradeoff is a heavier, stateful process (hangs, memory growth, stuck locks), trickier lifecycle in scripts and CI, and more work to make shutdown, signal handling, and concurrent sessions safe.\n\nIn practice, process-per-turn fits automation, batch jobs, and \u201cone shot per shell command\u201d; persistent stdin fits interactive REPL-style use where the human stays in one session and may interrupt or steer without restarting.","session_id":"11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111","request_id":"req-0001","usage":{"inputTokens":14414,"outputTokens":347,"cacheReadTokens":5749,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
